# Google Sheet Authentication (auto re-login)

## One place for credentials

Every sf-deploy script builds its Sheets/Drive client through
`sf-deploy/scripts/google_auth.py` (`sheets_service()`, `sheets_service(write=True)`,
`drive_service()`, `get_credentials()`). `fetch_sheet.get_sheets_service()` wraps
it. New scripts must use these helpers; never call `google.auth.default()` directly.

## What happens when the login has expired

Before each run the helper refreshes the token. If credentials are missing,
expired (`RefreshError` / "Reauthentication is needed"), or were created by
`gcloud auth application-default login` without a quota project, it backs up
`~/.config/gcloud/application_default_credentials.json` and runs:

```
gcloud auth login <account> --enable-gdrive-access --update-adc --force
```

A browser opens once; after sign-in the script continues on its own.
`gcloud` lives at `~/google-cloud-sdk/bin/gcloud` (not on PATH); the helper finds it.

## Agent behavior

1. Run sheet scripts with `required_permissions: ["full_network"]` as usual.
2. If a script stops with a Google auth error (`GoogleAuthError`, `RefreshError`,
 403 quota-project / `SERVICE_DISABLED`), do not stop the task. Run
 `python scripts/google_auth.py` with `required_permissions: ["all"]` (the
 login writes to `~/.config/gcloud` and opens a browser, which the sandbox
 blocks), tell the user to finish the sign-in in the browser, then re-run the
 original script.
3. Quick health check: `python scripts/google_auth.py --check` (no login).
 Force a fresh login: `python scripts/google_auth.py --login`.
4. Do NOT use `gcloud auth application-default login` — it fails with 403
 unless a quota project is set.
5. `SF_DEPLOY_NO_AUTO_LOGIN=1` makes scripts fail instead of opening a browser
 (for unattended runs). `GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON`, if set, is used first
 and never triggers a login.

The Cursor Google Workspace MCP has its own login and is not affected.
